{"uid":"b82807fc1441b296","name":"test_job_config_load_when_the_engine_uid_is_not_the_config_dir_owner","fullName":"tests.test_job_config_defaults#test_job_config_load_when_the_engine_uid_is_not_the_config_dir_owner","historyId":"a30d710eeaf8edf10114b51bbcfd2154","time":{"start":1791891473976,"stop":1791891473978,"duration":2},"description":"Task 023 (#180, requirement E.1), the uid mismatch itself.\n\n    The situation the requirement names cannot be built literally in the fast\n    tier -- an unprivileged test process cannot `chown` a directory to another\n    uid, and cannot change its own effective uid either (a real host-uid\n    mount is covered docker-tier by task 024/E.2). So the *difference* is\n    established here as the two numbers the kernel would compare, with the\n    comparison itself modelled: the config directory is owned by whoever runs\n    this test and chmod'ed 0700 exactly as `ralphctl start` leaves `/config`,\n    the engine's effective uid is declared to be a different number, and\n    `Path.exists`/`Path.read_text` under that directory answer the way the\n    kernel's DAC check answers for that pair -- denied for any effective uid\n    that is not the owner (a 0700 directory grants nothing to group or other),\n    exempt for uid 0. Two loads run against that: with no elevation the\n    mismatch alone hides `job.yaml` entirely (`Path.exists()` swallows EACCES,\n    so the job silently gets defaults -- the bug E.1 is about), and inside\n    `privsep.root_for_write()` the very same mismatch reads the file. `/config`\n    itself is asserted unchanged by the read: same owning uid, still 0700.\n    ","descriptionHtml":"<p>Task 023 (#180, requirement E.1), the uid mismatch itself.</p>\n<pre><code>The situation the requirement names cannot be built literally in the fast\ntier -- an unprivileged test process cannot `chown` a directory to another\nuid, and cannot change its own effective uid either (a real host-uid\nmount is covered docker-tier by task 024/E.2). So the *difference* is\nestablished here as the two numbers the kernel would compare, with the\ncomparison itself modelled: the config directory is owned by whoever runs\nthis test and chmod'ed 0700 exactly as `ralphctl start` leaves `/config`,\nthe engine's effective uid is declared to be a different number, and\n`Path.exists`/`Path.read_text` under that directory answer the way the\nkernel's DAC check answers for that pair -- denied for any effective uid\nthat is not the owner (a 0700 directory grants nothing to group or other),\nexempt for uid 0. Two loads run against that: with no elevation the\nmismatch alone hides `job.yaml` entirely (`Path.exists()` swallows EACCES,\nso the job silently gets defaults -- the bug E.1 is about), and inside\n`privsep.root_for_write()` the very same mismatch reads the file. `/config`\nitself is asserted unchanged by the read: same owning uid, still 0700.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"event_loop_policy","time":{"start":1791891119391,"stop":1791891119391,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"tmp_path_factory","time":{"start":1791891119392,"stop":1791891119392,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"monkeypatch","time":{"start":1791891473950,"stop":1791891473950,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"_isolate_ambient_home_in_process","time":{"start":1791891473950,"stop":1791891473959,"duration":9},"status":"passed","steps":[],